=== Ideals of the Ring $R_n lr((K,J))$ <sec:l2013-ideals-ring-ideals>

Throughout this paper the coefficient ring $K$ denotes an associative
commutative ring with identity.

Ideals of the ring $R_n lr((K,J))$ are described in
[@bib:l2013-ideals-Kuzucuoglu2000] for some specific ideal $J$ of $K$. The
description uses the following pair of sets of matrices positions:
$
  Lc={(i_1,j_1),(i_2,j_2),dots,(i_r,j_r)}, quad r>=1,
  quad 1<=j_1<j_2<dots<j_r<=n, quad 1<=i_1<i_2<dots<i_r<=n;
$ <eq:l2013-ideals-primary-corners>
$
  Lc'={(1,j_r),(k_1,m_1),(k_2,m_2),dots,(k_q,m_q),(i_1,n)}, quad q>=0,
  quad j_r<m_1<m_2<dots<m_q<=n, quad 1<=k_1<k_2<dots<k_q<i_1.
$ <eq:l2013-ideals-secondary-corners>

We refer to the ordered pair $(Lc,Lc')$ as a “set of corners of degree $n$”.

Denote by $pi_(k m)$ the canonical projection (see
[@bib:l2013-ideals-Hungerford1974]) on the ring of all $n times n$ matrices over
$K$ and by $e_(i j)$ the matrix unit in which the $(i,j)$-coefficient
$pi_(i j) lr((e_(i j)))$ is equal #source(3, printed: "1250140-3")to 1 and
others are zero. For any additive subgroup $F$ of $K$ we denote by
$N_(i j) lr((F))$ (respectively, by $Q_(i j) lr((F))$) the additive group of
$R_n lr((K,J))$ generated by sets $F e_(k m)$ for all $(k,m)>=(i,j)$
(respectively, $(k,m)>(i,j)$) where we suppose $(i,j)<(k,m)$, if $i<=k$, $m<=j$
and $(i,j)!=(k,m)$.

Also, we require the following concept.

#definition(title: [@bib:l2013-ideals-Kuzucuoglu2000, Definition~2.1])[
  An ideal $J$ of a commutative ring $K$ is called a strongly maximal ideal if
  for any $J$-submodule $T$ of $K$ every ideal of $K$, which is between a
  $J$-submodule $T$ and $J T$, is equal to $T$ or $J T$.
] <def:l2013-ideals-strongly-maximal>

Let $J$ be a strongly maximal ideal of $K$. In according to
[@bib:l2013-ideals-Kuzucuoglu2000, Theorem~2.2], every ideal of the ring
$R_n lr((K,J))$ is generated by a unique $T$-boundary
$A subset.eq R_n lr((K,J))$ which depends from a $J$-submodule $T$ of $K$ and
$(Lc,Lc')$ such that
$
  J B subset.eq A subset.eq B,
  quad B=sum_((i,j) in Lc) T e_(i j)+sum_((k,m) in Lc') (J T)e_(k m).
$
Moreover, $pi_(i j) lr((A))$ generates the ideal of $K$ coinciding with $J T$
for the free secondary corners and with $T$ at $(n,1)!=(i,j) in Lc$; if
$Lc={(n,1)}$, then $pi_(n 1) lr((A))=T$.

Regard a $n times n$ matrix as a square array of $n^2$ points $(i,j)$ (matrix
positions). Associating “staircases” with $Lc$ and $Lc'$ we can compare with the
ideal generated by the $T$-boundary $A$ the matrix in
Fig.~@fig:l2013-ideals-staircases.

By [@bib:l2013-ideals-Kuzucuoglu2000, Theorem~2.2], every ideal of the ring
$R_n lr((K,J))$ coincides with the ideal $I(A)$ which is generated by certain
$T$-boundary $A=A(T;Lc,Lc')$:
$
  I(A)=A+sum_((i,j) in Lc) Q_(i j) lr((T))+sum_((k,m) in Lc') Q_(k m) lr((J T))
  +N_(i_1 n) lr((T J))+N_(1 j_r) lr((J T))+N_(1 n) lr((J T J)).
$

